---
import Camera from "../components/Camera.astro";
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { SCENES } from "../scripts/film";

// The film sandwich, top (viewing surface) to bottom, as percentages of the
// stack's height. Runners climb from their home layer to the receiving layer,
// so every position here is also an animation target — keep them in sync with
// the --home/--dest values handed to each runner below.
const LAYERS = [
  { id: "receive", span: 14, name: "Receiving layer", note: "the picture forms here" },
  { id: "reagent", span: 20, name: "Reagent", note: "opaque at first, then white" },
  { id: "blue", span: 15, name: "Blue-sensitive", note: "yellow dye lives here" },
  { id: "green", span: 15, name: "Green-sensitive", note: "magenta dye lives here" },
  { id: "red", span: 15, name: "Red-sensitive", note: "cyan dye lives here" },
  { id: "base", span: 21, name: "Base", note: "opaque backing" },
];

// Exploded view: each layer slides along the stack's axis by its distance from
// the middle, so LAYERS' index is doing double duty as a position. Centring on
// (length - 1) / 2 keeps the stack's middle where it already is instead of
// dragging the whole diagram off one end.
const MID = (LAYERS.length - 1) / 2;
const offset = (id: string): number => LAYERS.findIndex((l) => l.id === id) - MID;

// Where each dye team starts: the vertical centre of its home layer, and the
// layer whose plane it sits on once the stack is pulled apart. A runner
// interpolates both together, so it flies out of its own layer and into the
// receiving layer rather than sliding along a single flat sheet.
const DEST = 7;
const TEAMS = [
  { id: "yellow", home: 41.5, from: "blue" },
  { id: "magenta", home: 56.5, from: "green" },
  { id: "cyan", home: 71.5, from: "red" },
];
const DEST_OFFSET = offset("receive");

// One dot per sample position down the column the slice marker is sitting on,
// so a lane in this diagram is a *place in the photograph* rather than a
// decorative repeat. Twelve is the most the 58% of width the lanes get can hold
// at three teams deep before the dots start hiding each other.
const PER_TEAM = 12;

// The dye progress bars under the print. Yellow, magenta and cyan here are not
// theme colours and never take the brand palette: they name which dye the bar
// is about, and a reader matching a bar to a dot in the diagram is matching on
// exactly that.
const BARS = [
  { id: "yellow", label: "Yellow dye" },
  { id: "magenta", label: "Magenta dye" },
  { id: "cyan", label: "Cyan dye" },
  { id: "clear", label: "Background clearing" },
];
---

<Layout title="A Polaroid develops backwards">
  {/* The five stripes are the one piece of Polaroid the reader recognises
      before they have read a word, so the page gets to borrow the recognition
      once, at full width, and then spend the rest of its colour budget on the
      diagram. Fixed rather than in flow: it is a letterhead, not a section. */}
  <div class="spectrum" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <header>
    <nav aria-label="Primary">
      <a href="./">Home</a>
    </nav>
  </header>

  <main>
    <h1>A Polaroid develops backwards</h1>

    <p class="lede">
      You have watched this happen. A photo slides out grey, and over ten
      minutes a picture swims up out of it. That is not what you were watching.
      The picture finished early. What took ten minutes was the
      <em>background</em> behind it turning from near-black to white.
    </p>

    <p>
      Drag the slider. Watch when the colour arrives, and when you are first
      allowed to see it.
    </p>

    {/* Above the film, not down in the sticky bar with the clock. The subject
        is chosen once and then watched; the clock is dragged while watching,
        and only that needs to follow you down the page. Keeping both in the
        bar made it 301px tall on a 390px phone — 36% of the screen spent on
        controls, most of them idle. Reading order agrees: point the camera at
        something, then look at the film. */}
    <fieldset class="scene">
      <legend class="scene__legend">What the camera was pointed at</legend>
      {
        SCENES.map((s, i) => (
          <label class="scene__opt">
            <input
              type="radio"
              name="scene"
              value={s.id}
              checked={i === 0}
              data-testid="scene"
            />
            <span>{s.label}</span>
          </label>
        ))
      }
    </fieldset>

    {/* The viewer comes first now, and it did not used to. When the right-hand
        column was a coloured square the diagram was the subject and the square
        was its result, so the diagram led. It is a camera now: you point it,
        press it, and a print comes out — and only then is there anything for a
        cross-section to be a cross-section *of*. The film's caption says "down
        the column the marker is on", and the marker lives in here, so meeting
        the marker second made that caption a forward reference. */}
    <div class="rig">
      {/* The frame is decoration with a job: a coloured square is a swatch,
          and the same square inside a Polaroid's white border is a photograph.
          The reader is being asked to believe the dark rectangle really is
          what they would be holding, and the frame is what makes that reading
          automatic instead of something the caption has to argue for. */}
      <figure class="viewer">
        <Camera />

        <div class="print" data-testid="print">
          <div class="polaroid">
            <div class="plate">
              <canvas
                class="photo"
                data-testid="photo"
                role="img"
                aria-label="The print, straight out of the camera: an almost black rectangle."
              ></canvas>

              {/* A line drawn into the canvas would be a line nobody can
                  reach. This is the same line as a DOM element sitting exactly
                  over the canvas, which costs one absolutely positioned div
                  and buys a tab stop, arrow keys and a focus ring. */}
              <div
                class="slice"
                data-testid="slice"
                tabindex="0"
                role="slider"
                aria-label="Which column of the photo the cross-section shows"
                aria-valuemin="0"
                aria-valuemax="100"
                aria-valuenow="50"
                aria-valuetext="the middle of the photo"
                style="--x:50"
              >
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <figcaption>What you see looking at the photo</figcaption>

        <ul class="bars" data-testid="bars">
          {
            BARS.map((bar) => (
              <li class="bars__row">
                <span class="bars__key">{bar.label}</span>
                <span class="bars__track">
                  <span class={`bars__fill bars__fill--${bar.id}`} data-bar={bar.id} />
                </span>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
      </figure>

      <figure class="film" data-testid="film">
        <div class="stack" aria-hidden="true">
          {
            LAYERS.map((layer) => (
              <div
                class={`layer layer--${layer.id}`}
                style={`--span:${layer.span}; --o:${offset(layer.id)}`}
              >
                <span class="layer__name">{layer.name}</span>
                <span class="layer__note">{layer.note}</span>
              </div>
            ))
          }

          <div class="runners">
            {
              TEAMS.flatMap((team, t) =>
                Array.from({ length: PER_TEAM }, (_, i) => (
                  <span
                    class={`runner runner--${team.id}`}
                    data-team={team.id}
                    style={`--home:${team.home}%; --dest:${DEST}%; --home-o:${
                      offset(team.from)
                    }; --dest-o:${DEST_OFFSET}; --lane:${
                      /* Lanes start right of the labels, and each team is
                         nudged sideways so arrivals stack visibly instead of
                         hiding behind whichever team painted last. The nudge
                         is a fraction of the lane pitch rather than a fixed
                         number, because the pitch moves when PER_TEAM does: a
                         flat 1.4% was measured overlapping the dots by half
                         their width at twelve samples, and anything past half
                         the pitch would put a team's dot nearer its
                         neighbour's lane than its own. 0.45 clears an 8px dot
                         at both marking widths and still reads as a column. */
                      39 + (i + (t - 1) * 0.45) * (56 / (PER_TEAM - 1))
                    }%; --lag:${i / (PER_TEAM * 2)}`}
                    data-i={i}
                  />
                )),
              )
            }
          </div>
        </div>
        <figcaption>
          A cross-section of the film, down the column the marker is on. Left
          to right here is top to bottom of that column.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="control">
      {/* The phone lays the rig out in a column, which puts the swatch off
          screen whenever the slider is on it — and the comparison between
          "the dye has arrived" and "the photo is still black" is the entire
          point. So the narrow viewport hides the big swatch and shows this
          one, riding in the bar beside the clock. Same value, one visible
          copy at any width. */}
      <div class="readout">
        <span class="readout__swatch" data-testid="photo-mini" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span class="readout__label">What you'd see</span>
        <p class="clock" data-testid="clock">0 seconds</p>
      </div>

      {/* 100 steps, not 1000. At max=1000 the slider dragged beautifully and
          was unusable by keyboard: one arrow press moved it 0.1%, so it took
          60 presses before anything on the page moved at all and hundreds to
          reach the part worth watching. 100 keeps the drag continuous — 3-4px
          a step across the track — while an arrow press is a visible move and
          Page Up/Down, which a native range gives for 10% free, crosses the
          whole thing in ten. */}
      <label class="scrub">
        <span class="scrub__label">Time since the photo left the camera</span>
        <input
          type="range"
          min="0"
          max="100"
          value="0"
          step="1"
          data-testid="time"
          aria-describedby="scrub-help"
        />
      </label>
      <p id="scrub-help" class="hint">
        Arrow keys step through it; Page Up and Page Down jump.
      </p>

      <label class="toggle">
        <input type="checkbox" id="explode" data-testid="explode" />
        <span>Tip the stack back and pull the layers apart</span>
      </label>

      {/* Pause only exists while there is something to pause. A dead control
          sitting there through the whole visit would be four more pixels of a
          bar this page has already fought to keep short.

          The status line is a live region and the callout over the camera is
          not, deliberately: the callout is timed to an animation, and an
          announcement timed to an animation is an announcement that arrives
          while the reader is still being told something else. */}
      <div class="playbar">
        <button type="button" class="playbar__btn" data-testid="pause" hidden>
          Pause
        </button>
        <button
          type="button"
          class="playbar__btn"
          data-testid="sound"
          aria-pressed="false"
        >
          Sound off
        </button>
        <p class="playbar__status" data-testid="status" role="status"></p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <section class="reading">
      <h2>What just happened</h2>
      <p>
        Each light-sensitive layer keeps its own colour of dye, and each dye
        molecule is also the chemical that develops that layer. When the rollers
        burst the reagent pod, every dye starts climbing toward the receiving
        layer at the top.
      </p>
      <p>
        A dye whose layer <em>was</em> hit by light has work to do. It does it,
        and in doing it, it gets stuck. A dye whose layer saw no light has
        nothing to do, so it climbs all the way up and stays there.
      </p>
      <p class="punch">
        So the picture is painted by the dye that had no work to do. Photograph
        something red, and it is the cyan that gets stuck — leaving magenta and
        yellow to arrive at the top, where they read as red.
      </p>
      <p>
        Change what the camera was pointed at and watch which team stalls. The
        two ends are the ones that prove it is a rule and not a trick: white
        light exposes every layer, so all three dyes have work, none of them
        arrive, and the print stays the white of the reagent's own pigment.
      </p>
      <p>
        Meanwhile the reagent that started the race is opaque, which is why the
        photo can leave the camera in daylight at all: it carries its own
        darkroom. An acid layer above it slowly neutralises it, and the dark
        grey-green goes white — revealing the white pigment that gives the print its
        background. The dye was in place long before you could see it.
      </p>
      <p class="aside">
        Shaking it does nothing. There is still liquid in there, and Polaroid
        asks you not to.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>

  <script src="../scripts/main.ts"></script>
</Layout>
